# Scroll Performance

The site targets 60 FPS during scroll. Every frame ≤ 16.7 ms. Profile-first: capture a Chrome DevTools Performance trace (4× CPU, Slow 4G, Screenshots ON) and apply a fix only where the trace shows the corresponding cost.

---

## 1. Pause animation libraries when offscreen

Lottie, GSAP, video, Three.js — none of them pause themselves. Wrap every instance with an `IntersectionObserver` that toggles `play()` / `pause()` on enter/exit. Keep the observer alive for the element's lifetime — do not `unobserve()` after first hit.

The project's Lottie wrapper at `src/js/custom/data-lottie.js` already does this. New Lottie containers only need the `data-lottie="/path/to/file.json"` attribute. Never call `lottie.loadAnimation()` directly in a block.

```html
<!-- ✅ Use the wrapper -->
<div
	class="lottie"
	data-lottie="/assets/lottie/foo.json"
	aria-hidden="true"
></div>
```

For other animation libs, follow the same pattern with a small (100–200 px) pre-warm `rootMargin`.

## 2. Pause infinite CSS animations when offscreen

Default any `animation: ... infinite` to `paused`. Toggle to `running` via an `.is-inview` class set by an `IntersectionObserver` on the section wrapper. Existing reference: `blocks/stats-strip/`.

```scss
// ✅ RIGHT — paused by default, only runs while in view
.{slug}-section {
	.track {
		animation: marquee 30s linear infinite;
		animation-play-state: paused;
		will-change: transform;
	}

	&.is-inview .track {
		animation-play-state: running;
	}
}

// ❌ WRONG — runs forever, costs CPU even when offscreen
.{slug}-section .track {
	animation: marquee 30s linear infinite;
}
```

Companion JS in `blocks/{slug}/{slug}.js`:

```javascript
const observer = new IntersectionObserver(
	entries => {
		entries.forEach(entry => {
			entry.target.classList.toggle('is-inview', entry.isIntersecting);
		});
	},
	{ rootMargin: '100px 0px' }
);
sections.forEach(section => observer.observe(section));
```

## 3. Serialize heavy library inits

When multiple animation instances may enter the viewport in the same scroll batch, do not initialize them all at once — microtask pile-up causes seconds-long blocking tasks. The Lottie wrapper already serializes inits one per frame, gated on bodymovin's `DOMLoaded`. Apply the same pattern to any new library that builds a heavy DOM tree on init.

```javascript
// ✅ RIGHT — process one per frame, wait for ready event
const queue = [];
let processing = false;

const processQueue = () => {
	if (processing || !queue.length) return;
	processing = true;
	const el = queue.shift();
	initLibrary(el, () => {
		requestAnimationFrame(() => {
			processing = false;
			processQueue();
		});
	});
};

// ❌ WRONG — pre-resolved Promises pile into one microtask flush
paths.forEach(p => {
	cache.set(
		p,
		fetch(p).then(r => r.json())
	);
});
// later: cached.then(initLibrary)  // all .then callbacks fire back-to-back
```

## 4. Never measure layout inside a RAF loop

Calling `getBoundingClientRect()`, `offsetTop`, `offsetHeight`, `clientHeight`, or `window.innerHeight` inside `requestAnimationFrame` forces synchronous layout every frame. This is the most common scroll-jank root cause.

**Pattern for scroll-driven progress effects** (existing reference: `blocks/big-heading/big-heading.js`):

```javascript
// ✅ RIGHT — cache geometry on init/resize, derive from scrollY
let vh = window.innerHeight;
const cache = new WeakMap();

const measure = el => {
	const rect = el.getBoundingClientRect();
	cache.set(el, { docTop: rect.top + window.scrollY, height: rect.height });
};

const update = el => {
	const c = cache.get(el);
	const top = c.docTop - window.scrollY; // pure math, no DOM read
	// ...derive progress from top, vh, c.height
};

let rafId = null;
const schedule = () => {
	if (rafId !== null) return;
	rafId = requestAnimationFrame(() => {
		rafId = null;
		visible.forEach(update);
	});
};

window.addEventListener('scroll', schedule, { passive: true });
window.addEventListener(
	'resize',
	() => {
		vh = window.innerHeight;
		headings.forEach(measure);
	},
	{ passive: true }
);
```

```javascript
// ❌ WRONG — getBoundingClientRect every frame forces layout
const tick = () => {
	visible.forEach(el => {
		const rect = el.getBoundingClientRect(); // layout-thrash
		const vh = window.innerHeight; // also forces layout
		// ...
	});
	requestAnimationFrame(tick);
};
requestAnimationFrame(tick);
```

Also: the update should run **once per scroll event batch**, not on a continuous RAF loop. Continuous RAF burns CPU even when the user isn't scrolling.

## 5. `will-change` is surgical, not blanket

Add `will-change: transform` only on elements the trace shows as composite-churning (e.g. an actively transforming marquee track — see `blocks/stats-strip/stats-strip.scss`). Over-promotion costs GPU memory. Never apply site-wide.

## 6. Image perf — see `image-assets.mdc`

Non-LCP `<img>` get `loading="lazy" decoding="async"` plus explicit `width` / `height`. The above-the-fold logo / hero stays eager.

## 7. What NOT to change without a profile

- Lenis config (`wheelMultiplier`, `lerp`, `syncTouch`) — touching this changes feel.
- Animation timings, easing, durations — visible behavior changes.
- Lottie renderer (SVG → canvas) — visual quality changes.
- Replacing animations with simpler equivalents — design changes.

These are out-of-scope for a perf pass unless explicitly approved.
